WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.325
<v Jamie>Walmart stock just tanked seven point three nine percent because the company wants to give you cheaper groceries—and Wall Street is genuinely mad about it.

00:00:09.325 --> 00:00:42.524
<v Alex>S&P down zero point three three percent, Nasdaq down zero point four eight percent, and the Russell two thousand actually up zero point one six percent—which is the market's way of saying "we're scared of big tech but we'll take whatever we can get." Walmart lost seven point three nine percent. Adobe down three point eight six percent. Costco down two point six six percent. Meanwhile Cisco jumped two point one eight percent, which tells you everything: investors are fleeing growth and hugging infrastructure like it's a life raft.

00:00:42.524 --> 00:00:52.222
<v Jamie>So Walmart tanks because it wants to help people afford groceries, and Cisco moons because—what, they sell the boring pipes that hold the internet together?

00:00:52.222 --> 00:01:20.944
<v Alex>Exactly. Walmart chose violence today against its own margin. Cisco is basically the Crocs IPO of corporate governance—nobody's excited about it, but it works, it's profitable, and apparently that's enough to make money right now. The V I X dropped to seventeen point three five, so macro anxiety eased, but earnings reality is biting. Tech and retail are both getting their teeth kicked in because the market finally realized growth stocks need to, you know, grow.

00:01:20.944 --> 00:01:36.611
<v Maya>Wait, so Cisco is just—it's the safe choice? That's the trade? We're in a world where A M D is down two percent and Cisco is up because it's boring? That's not a market, that's a nursing home portfolio.

00:01:36.611 --> 00:01:58.992
<v Alex>Welcome to 2025, where the exciting part of the stock market is watching companies that sell switches to data centers. Netflix up one point three three percent, Merck up one point three nine percent, Eli Lilly up one point five eight percent—these are your winners. You know what they have in common? None of them promised to disrupt anything.

00:01:58.992 --> 00:02:16.524
<v Jamie>So the Walmart thing—that stock has commitment issues. They say "we want lower prices," the market says "no," they panic. Meanwhile Cisco's just sitting there like "I will do the same thing I did in two thousand and three" and Wall Street throws money at it.

00:02:16.524 --> 00:02:43.008
<v Maya>Okay so SpaceX just filed its S-one for the Nasdaq listing, and the filing is basically screaming one thing: Starlink is not a side project, Starlink is the entire business. Like, Starlink generated the majority of SpaceX's revenue growth, and the prospectus literally says the company is "dependent on Starlink for growth and profitability." Elon's not going to Mars on rocket money—he's going to Mars on satellite internet money.

00:02:43.008 --> 00:02:54.945
<v Jamie>Wait, so the company that's supposed to be about launching rockets to space is actually making its money by... beaming the internet from space back down to your house in Nebraska?

00:02:54.945 --> 00:03:17.699
<v Alex>Yes. SpaceX's core business—the thing that gets venture capitalists and defense contractors excited—is the Falcon nine and Falcon Heavy launches. But Starlink is where the cash is. The filing doesn't give exact numbers, but the rocket business is still burning money on development and government contracts. This is what happens when your founder gets bored with the original mission.

00:03:17.699 --> 00:03:31.874
<v Maya>It's like if Tesla filed for IPO and the prospectus said "by the way, we make most of our money selling solar panels to Costco, not cars." Investors bought the Mars story. They're getting the Starlink story.

00:03:31.874 --> 00:03:41.199
<v Jamie>But hold on—is that actually bad? Like, if Starlink is profitable and growing, why would Wall Street care that it's not the rocket division?

00:03:41.199 --> 00:04:04.326
<v Alex>Because Wall Street bought a ticket to Mars and got a fiber optic cable. Starlink is a telecommunications business. It has competition—Amazon's got Project Kuiper launching soon, and unlike Elon, Amazon actually has the cash to burn. Starlink has a foothold. And the prospectus basically admits the company's future depends on whether Starlink can scale faster than Amazon can copy it.

00:04:04.326 --> 00:04:25.962
<v Maya>Plus there's the whole thing where Starlink is also SpaceX's biggest customer for launches. Like, SpaceX launches Starlink satellites, Starlink pays SpaceX for the launches, and then SpaceX reports both as revenue. It's not circular exactly, but it's definitely... cozy. The filing has to disclose that Starlink accounted for over fifty percent of SpaceX's launch revenue.

00:04:25.962 --> 00:04:33.422
<v Jamie>So when SpaceX goes public, we're basically buying a bet on satellite internet, not a bet on Mars rockets?

00:04:33.422 --> 00:05:04.382
<v Alex>You're buying a bet on whether Elon can execute two businesses at once without one of them imploding. The rocket division is government-dependent—it's got contracts with the Air Force, N A S A, the Space Force. That's stable. But it's also heavily regulated and capped by how many launches the government needs. Starlink is the growth story. Starlink is also the thing that could get crushed by Amazon, or by regulatory pushback. The filing doesn't mention the collision problem, but it's there.

00:05:04.382 --> 00:05:34.224
<v Maya>And here's the thing—the IPO is valued at a significant amount or more, right? That valuation is built on Starlink being the next generation of internet infrastructure. But if you strip out Starlink, SpaceX is just a launch provider. A really good one, but a launch provider. The margins on that are okay, not transformational. You're not going public on "we launch rockets reliably." You're going public on "we're going to wire the entire planet with internet from space."

00:05:34.224 --> 00:05:47.653
<v Jamie>So Elon's basically saying, "I know I said I was going to colonize Mars, but first let me make sure everyone in rural Wyoming can stream TikTok." That's not a pivot, that's a full one-eighty.

00:05:47.653 --> 00:06:00.708
<v Alex>It's the Crocs IPO of space companies. Nobody thought Starlink would be the profit engine. Everyone thought it was the cool vanity project. Turns out the vanity project is the only thing that works.

00:06:00.708 --> 00:06:16.002
<v Maya>Anthropic is paying fifteen billion dollars a year for access to data centers while SpaceX is literally filing to go public right now. This is the Crocs IPO of corporate governance. The CEO's side hustle becomes the main character.
  ↳ source: https://www.theverge.com/science/935229/spacex-anthropic-ipo-ai-capacity-deal-colossus

00:06:16.002 --> 00:06:25.327
<v Jamie>Wait, so Anthropic needs compute to train Claude, and they're basically funding infrastructure while a company is about to ask public markets for capital?

00:06:25.327 --> 00:06:47.335
<v Alex>Yes. Anthropic just handed a company fifteen billion a year in guaranteed revenue—which can now be put in the prospectus as "recurring enterprise customer revenue." The filing doesn't need to say "we have one customer paying us fifteen billion," but it can imply the data center business is scaled and real. The funding helped the IPO story.
  ↳ source: https://www.theverge.com/science/935229/spacex-anthropic-ipo-ai-capacity-deal-colossus

00:06:47.335 --> 00:06:58.526
<v Maya>It's like if you lent your friend money to buy a house, and then he immediately listed the house on Zillow and told the bank "see, I have income."

00:06:58.526 --> 00:07:14.566
<v Alex>Air France and Airbus were just convicted of manslaughter by a French court over a plane crash—two hundred twenty-eight people died, and it took years to get a guilty verdict. Turns out "too big to fail" doesn't mean "too big to prosecute."
  ↳ source: https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/czd2qmdvmq6o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss

00:07:14.566 --> 00:07:21.653
<v Jamie>So a court actually held a major airline and a major aircraft manufacturer criminally liable? That doesn't happen.

00:07:21.653 --> 00:07:45.899
<v Alex>It happened in France. The court found Air France negligent for not detecting a known issue with the air speed sensor, and Airbus negligent for not warning operators about the sensor's failure mode. They each got fined five million euros. Which for Airbus is what they make in like forty-five minutes, but symbolically it's real. European regulators decided accountability matters more than shareholder comfort.
  ↳ source: https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/czd2qmdvmq6o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss

00:07:45.899 --> 00:07:52.614
<v Maya>That's the opposite of what happens here. We crash planes, we write a check, we move on.

00:07:52.614 --> 00:08:06.415
<v Alex>Spotify stock popped after their first investor day in four years—they're guiding for operating margin expansion and profitability. The streaming wars are over. The winner is the company that convinced Wall Street podcasts are a moat.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/05/21/spotify-spot-stock-guidance-investor-day-ai.html

00:08:06.415 --> 00:08:13.876
<v Jamie>Podcasts? Spotify's moat is podcasts? Not music, not the algorithm, but the thing where people talk into a microphone?

00:08:13.876 --> 00:08:34.392
<v Maya>They own Spotify Studios, they've got exclusive deals with major creators—it's like they looked at Netflix's content spend and said "what if we did that but cheaper because people are less picky about audio quality." And it worked. Podcast listeners are stickier than music listeners because they're subscribed to a person, not a song.

00:08:34.392 --> 00:08:50.804
<v Alex>Spotify convinced investors that the future of audio entertainment is exclusive podcasts. And they're right. Music streaming is a commodity now—Apple Music, Amazon Music, YouTube Music all have the same catalog. Podcasts are exclusive. That's the defensible business. The guidance bump is real.

00:08:50.804 --> 00:09:01.622
<v Maya>The White House just postponed the AI executive order signing ceremony—you know, the big photo op. Nothing says "we have a coherent AI strategy" like canceling the ceremony.

00:09:01.622 --> 00:09:06.098
<v Jamie>They're not signing it at all, or they're just—moving the event?

00:09:06.098 --> 00:09:25.495
<v Maya>Moving it. Which means either the order isn't done, or they realized signing it on camera was a bad idea. Either way, it's noise. The E O was supposed to be about AI safety and cybersecurity, but if you can't even get the signing ceremony right, what are we doing here?

00:09:25.495 --> 00:09:29.598
<v Alex>This is regulatory theater canceling its own performance. Move on.

00:09:29.598 --> 00:09:54.591
<v Maya>Okay, so five people were arrested in a council election fraud investigation. No details yet on what they actually did, but the fact that police are holding people for council election fraud is wild because, like, who commits fraud to win a seat on the local planning committee? That's not a crime of passion, that's a crime of "I really want to argue about potholes."

00:09:54.591 --> 00:10:03.543
<v Jamie>Wait, council elections are like... local government, right? What's the upside? You get to sit in a room and fight about bus routes?

00:10:03.543 --> 00:10:17.345
<v Alex>The upside is zero. The downside is five people are now facing criminal charges because they wanted a position that pays nothing and requires you to attend meetings. This is regulatory theater arresting its own audience.

00:10:17.345 --> 00:10:27.789
<v Maya>It's the most British crime possible. In America, election fraud is about power and money. In Britain, it's about getting a badge that lets you complain officially.

00:10:27.789 --> 00:10:56.885
<v Alex>Walmart's tariff refund gamble is the only move that actually made sense—they're eating the cost instead of passing it to customers, which means they're betting that loyalty beats margin expansion. Most CEOs would've taken the tariff as permission to raise prices and pocket the difference. Walmart looked at that playbook and said "no thanks, we're going to be the airline that doesn't charge for carry-ons." It won't last, but for now it's the weirdest flex in retail.

00:10:56.885 --> 00:11:15.163
<v Maya>They're basically saying "we're so confident in our volume that we can afford to be the good guy for a while," which is the corporate equivalent of someone actually returning a shopping cart in a parking lot—theoretically possible but so rare you have to comment on it.

00:11:15.163 --> 00:11:20.385
<v Jamie>This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
